Support the WCH CH390 with the Davicom DM9051 driver, and fix three defects
that kept it from passing traffic.

The CH390 is a 10/100 MAC and PHY in one SPI part whose register map is the
DM9051's. Its identity registers are not: it reports vendor 0x1C00 and product
0x9151 where eth_dm9051_check_id() demands 0x9051, so the driver rejects it at
init and the interface never appears. Rather than duplicate a register
compatible driver, the expected product ID is carried per instance and the
instance macro runs once per compatible, the way drivers/ethernet/eth_adin2111.c
already serves the ADIN2111 and the ADIN1110.

Accepting the part was not enough to make it work. Three further defects,
each found and then confirmed fixed on an Elecrow ThinkNode M7 with a CH390:

1. The soft reset in hw_start() returns GPR to its reset default, which on
   this part leaves the PHY powered down. MDIO still answers, so the PHY
   reports itself healthy, auto-negotiation-capable and advertising 10/100,
   while the line side stays dark: auto-negotiation never completes and the
   link partner ability register stays empty. Re-asserting GPR after the reset
   brings the link up.

2. The pre-transmit NSR poll waits for a TX-end bit that is only set once a
   transmit has completed, and treats its timeout as fatal. On a freshly
   started chip no transmit has ever completed, so every frame is aborted
   before a byte is written, and TX-end can never be set because no transmit
   ever happens. The interface silently transmits nothing. With this fixed the
   node completes DHCP.

3. The receive thread waits on the interrupt alone, but the pin is
   edge-triggered while the controller drives it from a level. A new event
   setting ISR before the thread clears it leaves the line asserted with no
   fresh edge, and receive stalls permanently. The node completed DHCP and
   then answered neither ICMP nor TCP. Bounding the wait leaves interrupts as
   the fast path and makes the stall self-healing.

Defects 2 and 3 are not CH390-specific and look like they affect the DM9051
equally; no DM9051 board exists in-tree to confirm that against.

4. An RX status error restarted the whole controller. hw_start() resets the
   MAC and the PHY, so it drops the carrier and forces a renegotiation. The
   status bit that actually fires is the FIFO overflow, which is ordinary
   congestion on a busy segment, so every busy moment cost a carrier loss, a
   link renegotiation and a fresh DHCP cycle: measured three link bounces in
   ten minutes under a 5 packet per second ping. A frame whose length is
   usable is now skipped in the FIFO and reception continues; the restart is
   kept only for a length outside the legal range, where the read pointer
   cannot be trusted and there is nothing to skip past. The same ten-minute
   load then produced one dropped frame and no link event at all.

Verified on hardware: link up at 100 Mbps, DHCP lease acquired, ping 3/3 at
1.9 ms average and the companion's TCP port reachable from another host.

The binding lives in patches/zephyr-new/dts/bindings/ethernet/wch,ch390.yaml.
`wch` is already a registered vendor prefix upstream.

diff --git a/drivers/ethernet/Kconfig.dm9051 b/drivers/ethernet/Kconfig.dm9051
--- a/drivers/ethernet/Kconfig.dm9051
+++ b/drivers/ethernet/Kconfig.dm9051
@@ -4,12 +4,13 @@
 # SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
 
 menuconfig ETH_DM9051
-	bool "Davicom DM9051 Ethernet Controller"
+	bool "Davicom DM9051 / WCH CH390 Ethernet Controller"
 	default y
-	depends on DT_HAS_DAVICOM_DM9051_ENABLED
+	depends on DT_HAS_DAVICOM_DM9051_ENABLED || DT_HAS_WCH_CH390_ENABLED
 	select SPI
 	help
-	  Davicom DM9051 Ethernet Controller with SPI Interface
+	  Davicom DM9051 Ethernet Controller with SPI Interface, and the
+	  register-compatible WCH CH390.
 
 config ETH_DM9051_RX_THREAD_STACK_SIZE
 	int "Stack size for internal incoming packet handler"
diff --git a/drivers/ethernet/eth_dm9051.c b/drivers/ethernet/eth_dm9051.c
--- a/drivers/ethernet/eth_dm9051.c
+++ b/drivers/ethernet/eth_dm9051.c
@@ -36,6 +36,10 @@
 /* DM9051 Product ID */
 #define DM9051_ID			0x9051
 
+/* CH390 Product ID. WCH's part is register-compatible with the DM9051 but
+ * reports its own identity (vendor 0x1C00), so only the ID gate differs. */
+#define CH390_ID			0x9151
+
 /* DM9051 PHY Address */
 #define DM9051_PHY_ADDR			1
 
@@ -177,6 +181,7 @@
 struct eth_dm9051_config {
 	struct net_eth_mac_config mac_cfg;
 	struct gpio_dt_spec gpio_int;
+	uint16_t chip_id;
 	const struct device *phy_dev;
 	struct spi_dt_spec spi;
 };
@@ -313,6 +318,7 @@
 
 static int eth_dm9051_check_id(const struct device *dev)
 {
+	const struct eth_dm9051_config *config = dev->config;
 	uint16_t pid;
 	int ret;
 
@@ -323,8 +329,9 @@
 	}
 
 	/* Check product ID */
-	if (sys_le16_to_cpu(pid) != DM9051_ID) {
-		LOG_ERR("%s: Found ID: %04x, Expected ID: %04x\n", dev->name, pid, DM9051_ID);
+	if (sys_le16_to_cpu(pid) != config->chip_id) {
+		LOG_ERR("%s: Found ID: %04x, Expected ID: %04x\n", dev->name, pid,
+			config->chip_id);
 		return -EIO;
 	}
 
@@ -399,6 +406,22 @@
 
 	k_usleep(10);
 
+	/*
+	 * Re-assert the PHY power-on. The soft reset above returns GPR to its
+	 * reset default, and on the CH390 that default leaves the PHY powered
+	 * down: MDIO keeps answering, so the PHY reports itself healthy and
+	 * auto-negotiation-capable, while the line side stays dark and the link
+	 * never comes up. Measured on an Elecrow ThinkNode M7, where the link
+	 * partner's ability register stayed empty until this write was added.
+	 * The 100 ms settle is what WCH's own driver allows.
+	 */
+	ret = eth_dm9051_spi_write_reg(dev, DM9051_GPR, DM9051_GPR_PHY_ON);
+	if (ret < 0) {
+		return ret;
+	}
+
+	k_msleep(100);
+
 	/* Set gpio pin polarity based on gpio dt flags */
 	ret = eth_dm9051_spi_write_reg(dev, DM9051_INTCR,
 				       (config->gpio_int.dt_flags & GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW) > 0 ?
@@ -451,8 +474,16 @@
 
 	k_mutex_lock(&data->spi_lock, K_FOREVER);
 
+	/*
+	 * Best-effort wait for an in-flight transmit; a timeout is not an error.
+	 * NSR's TX-end bits stay clear until a transmit has completed, so on a
+	 * freshly started chip this poll never passes, and treating that as a
+	 * failure aborts every frame before a byte is written. TX-end can then
+	 * never be set, because no transmit ever happens, and the interface
+	 * silently transmits nothing for the life of the boot.
+	 */
 	ret = eth_dm9051_nsr_poll(dev, DM9051_NSR_POLL_TIMEOUT);
-	if (ret < 0) {
+	if (ret < 0 && ret != -ETIMEDOUT) {
 		goto out_spi_unlock;
 	}
 
@@ -506,17 +537,16 @@
 
 	rx_len = sys_get_le16(rxhdr.len);
 
-	/* Check for RX errors */
-	if ((rxhdr.status & ~DM9051_RSR_MF) > 0 ||
-	    !IN_RANGE(rx_len, ETH_DM9051_MIN_FRAME_SIZE, sizeof(data->rx_buf))) {
-		if ((rxhdr.status & ~DM9051_RSR_MF) > 0) {
-			LOG_DBG("%s: RX status error: %02x", dev->name, rxhdr.status);
-		}
-
-		if (!IN_RANGE(rx_len, ETH_DM9051_MIN_FRAME_SIZE, sizeof(data->rx_buf))) {
-			LOG_DBG("%s: RX length out of range: %u (min: %u, max: %zu)",
-				dev->name, rx_len, ETH_DM9051_MIN_FRAME_SIZE, sizeof(data->rx_buf));
-		}
+	/*
+	 * A length outside the legal range means the FIFO read pointer can no
+	 * longer be trusted: there is no way to know where this frame ends, so
+	 * nothing can be skipped and the controller has to be restarted. That
+	 * restart resets the MAC and the PHY and therefore drops the carrier,
+	 * so it is reserved for exactly this case.
+	 */
+	if (!IN_RANGE(rx_len, ETH_DM9051_MIN_FRAME_SIZE, sizeof(data->rx_buf))) {
+		LOG_DBG("%s: RX length out of range: %u (min: %u, max: %zu)",
+			dev->name, rx_len, ETH_DM9051_MIN_FRAME_SIZE, sizeof(data->rx_buf));
 
 		ret = eth_dm9051_hw_start(dev, data->iface);
 		if (ret < 0) {
@@ -525,6 +555,25 @@
 		return NULL;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * A status error with a usable length is a frame to drop, not a reason
+	 * to restart the controller. The common one is a FIFO overflow, which
+	 * is ordinary congestion on a busy segment; restarting there turns a
+	 * dropped frame into a carrier loss, a link renegotiation and a fresh
+	 * DHCP cycle every time the segment gets busy. The length is
+	 * trustworthy here, so the frame is skipped in the FIFO instead and
+	 * reception continues.
+	 */
+	if ((rxhdr.status & ~DM9051_RSR_MF) > 0) {
+		LOG_DBG("%s: RX status error: %02x, dropping frame", dev->name, rxhdr.status);
+
+		ret = eth_dm9051_spi_read_mem(dev, DM9051_MRCMD, NULL, rx_len);
+		if (ret < 0) {
+			LOG_ERR("%s: Failed to discard RX data (err %d)", dev->name, ret);
+		}
+		return NULL;
+	}
+
 	/* Alloc RX net_pkt and subtract 4 from RX length to discard CRC */
 	pkt = net_pkt_rx_alloc_with_buffer(data->iface,
 					   rx_len - ETH_DM9051_CRC_SIZE,
@@ -679,7 +728,17 @@
 	data = dev->data;
 
 	while (true) {
-		k_sem_take(&data->int_event, K_FOREVER);
+		/*
+		 * Wait for the interrupt, but never only for it. The pin is
+		 * edge-triggered while the controller drives it from a level:
+		 * when a new event sets ISR before the thread clears it, the
+		 * line stays asserted, no fresh edge arrives and receive stalls
+		 * for good. Observed on a CH390, where the interface completed
+		 * DHCP and then answered nothing. The poll bounds that stall
+		 * instead of leaving it permanent; interrupts still deliver
+		 * immediately when they arrive.
+		 */
+		(void)k_sem_take(&data->int_event, K_MSEC(50));
 
 		ret = eth_dm9051_spi_read_reg(dev, DM9051_ISR, &isr);
 		if (ret < 0) {
@@ -987,24 +1046,32 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
